speechtext:
I shall not take more than a few minutes. I agree with the previous speaker (Mr. Euler), that these public utilities ought to be controlled by the state. Who own the coal-beds in the United States? The railways themselves, which are private corporations. We are getting railroads in this country which will be a national asset, and we must take hold of our fuel problem. If the people of the United States are paying such enormous prices to-day for coal, it is because the railway monopolies made a monopoly of the coal mines;

and, as the previous speaker stated, we must avoid that. One thing I want to point out is the fact that transportation must he a matter of the electrification of our railways, and we can start with the development of the St. Lawrence powers. We can obtain power enough there, and also in Ontario, to carry cars up to the head of the lakes, and the coal supplies of the Maritime Provinces can be used to electrify the railways there by burning the coal at the pit mouth. They are coming to that in England, where they see great improvements in that direction. It is also possible, by the use of our river-powers, to get almost sufficient power to drive all the railways from one coast to the other. We should have more cars for transportation of coal. I think it is a good policy in connection with this question of coal to ascertain at the same time how much power we can get by the use of the water-power in our rivers, and also by the generation of power at the pit mouth in the mines. In that way we could get enough power to carry coal across the continent, and so avoid burning up 70 per cent of the coal we bring into the country in driving engines on the railways. The problem is a very wide one. It goes into the question of the use of electricity developed both from coal at the pit mouth and from power generated by the rivers, and it involves also the necessity of developing the greatest possible amount of power we can. Supposing we are dependent on the United States for anthracite coal, and particularly for coal for Ontario and Quebec, if we developed the electrical power which has already been referred to in this House, and obtained the highest possible amount of it, should the Americans be wanting power, we could trade our power for their coal, and in that way the balance of the account might be in our favour. So that all these questions must be taken up in connection with the fuel problem-the question of power, the question of transportation and the utilization of our surplus water-power-if we have a surplus of power, sending some of it to the Americans in return for a friendly supply of coal from them, thus relieving us from the necessity of having to beg it from them. So I commend to the consideration of the Government the proposal before the House. I hope the Government will appoint a committee of the character that has been suggested, that a full study of the whole question will be made, and that one result will be to ensure to the
people of this country a plentiful supply of coal. Speaking of coal, I hope that the news coming from the Mackenzie Basin in the Northwest is correct. I trust a great supply of petroleum will be found there, and that it will help in the solution of the fuel problem of Canada.
